{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-4I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a635abd641512581d5b502fcd2318fc136a25a6af9112bbae6bb4d3a383af011"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-36929" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-37634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-37634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )-8\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-37634" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a635abd641512581d5b502fcd2318fc136a25a6af9112bbae6bb4d3a383af011"} {"question" "q-37634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-37634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$B$ is not diagonalizable"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-37634" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a635abd641512581d5b502fcd2318fc136a25a6af9112bbae6bb4d3a383af011"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a635abd641512581d5b502fcd2318fc136a25a6af9112bbae6bb4d3a383af011"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-80467" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-140040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-140040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -32+36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-2I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-140040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a635abd641512581d5b502fcd2318fc136a25a6af9112bbae6bb4d3a383af011"} {"question" "q-140040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-140040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["text" "$A$ is not diagonalizable"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097693cf95d091cd5504051cecffe2a06a4e"} {"question" "q-140040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ is not diagonalizable"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14060154/state.json" "file_sha256" "282c262acee306f3fc6456377b02097